FFmpeg: libavcodec, libavformat, libavutil, libswresample
  Copyright the FFmpeg developers
  GNU Lesser General Public License 2.1 or later:
  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html
  Source: https://ffmpeg.org/releases/
  The exact version, its checksum and the complete configure line are in
  scripts/build-ffmpeg.sh, which is what produced the binaries and is sufficient
  to reproduce them.

  Built WITHOUT --enable-gpl and WITHOUT --enable-nonfree, so no GPL component
  (libpostproc, the three GPL x86 optimisation files, the GPL filters) and no
  nonfree component (Fraunhofer FDK AAC, OpenSSL) is present. x264 and x265 are
  not linked. A test reads the licence and the configure line back out of the
  built library, so this paragraph cannot quietly stop being true.

  DYNAMICALLY LINKED. The four FFmpeg libraries are shipped as separate shared
  libraries beside Limn's shim (liblimnffmpeg), never statically linked into it,
  so they can be replaced with a modified build without relinking anything,
  which is the freedom the LGPL exists to protect. See docs/adr/011.

  No FFmpeg binary is in this repository. The libraries a published jar carries
  are produced by the release build from the source and the configure line named
  above, packaged into the jar for every desktop target, and the licence text and
  this notice travel in the jar beside them: a jar's reader has no repository to
  look in. A developer who wants them locally fetches that payload with
  scripts/fetch-ffmpeg.sh or builds it with scripts/build-ffmpeg.sh; without one,
  the decoder reports itself unavailable and nothing else changes.

  Applications that do not play video can exclude limn-video-ffmpeg entirely; it
  is a separate module and nothing else depends on it.

